    "evidence_text": "Bidirectional LSTM (BLSTM) can be regarded as two separate LSTMs with different directions.\n\nThe sampling strategy is Bernoulli distribution described in  .\n\nDatasets\tWe use two popular knowledge bases: WordNet  and Freebase  in this paper.\n\nTable 1 lists statistics\n\nof the two datasets.\n\nSimilar to  , we use two measures as our evaluation metrics.\n\nWe call this evaluation setting \u201cRaw\u201d.\n\nWe call this evaluation setting \u201cFilter\u201d.\n\nThe evaluation results are reported under these two settings.\n\nWe select the margin\n\nOur baseline is TransE since that the score function of our models is based on TransE.\n\nOn FB15K, \u201cJointly(A-LSTM)\u201d achieves the best\n\nperformance and is significantly higher than baseline methods on mean rank.\n\nOur models are compatible with other state-of-the-art knowledge embedding models.\n\nMapping properties of relations follows the same rules in  .\n\nWe can see that our models outperform baseline TransE\n\nFigure 3 gives the average of gates in ten groups from high- to low-frequency.\n\nWe observe that the text information play more important role for the low-frequency entities.\n\nFor triplets classification, we set a threshold $\\delta _r$ for each relation\n\n$r$ .\n\n$\\delta _r$ is obtained by maximizing the classification accuracies on the valid set.\n\nTable 4 shows the evaluation results of triplets classification.\n\nThe results reveal that our joint encoding models is effective and also outperform the baseline method.\n\nThe reason is that the number of relations is relatively small.\n\nTherefore, the attention mechanism does not show obvious advantage.\n\njointly embed knowledge and text into the",
